    "body": "When a parent is accepted on its own, each orphan spending it is queued for reconsideration with **one**\r\nrandomly chosen announcer. If that announcer disconnects, or its announcement is evicted, before the\r\norphan is processed, the orphan is never retried, even though other announcers remain and its inputs are\r\nnow available. Later announcements don't help, since the orphan is already held. The child (for example\r\na CPFP of a parent that pays for itself) stays out of the mempool until the orphan is evicted, and an\r\nannouncer can cause this on purpose by disconnecting. This is R2 in\r\nhttps://gist.github.com/instagibbs/880a76535fd802fced9f9a5253cdbb40. This is a narrow hole, albeit real\r\n\r\nThe first commit hands reconsideration over to a remaining announcer when the marked announcement is\r\nerased.\r\n\r\nThe second commit adds `p2p_1p1c_liveness`, an end-to-end fuzz target for parent and child relay. It\r\ndrives a real PeerManager with real messages and validation, and checks that if an honest peer announces\r\nthe child of an acceptable parent and child, the child reaches the mempool within a bounded time,\r\nwhatever other peers do. An earlier version found this bug\r\n(https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36371#issuecomment-5937814761); this version finds it on master\r\nfrom an empty corpus in under an hour on one core.\r\n\r\nThe harness also labels certain sequence of events that would result in 1p1c package resolution failure.\r\nThese are \"holes\" in the stated invariant we want to plug in future work, potentially such as #36369. Cataloging\r\nthem here means we can discover new ones as campaigns happen, and remove labels as they are fixed.\r\nSome such as `parent_left_with_orphan` may not be worth filling, but are documented in the harness for completeness\r\nand to not cause crashes when encountered.\r\n",
